> fourier series sign(x)
Fourier series of sign(x) on [-pi, pi] (period 2pi):
  f(x) ~ a0/2 + sum_{n>=1} a_n cos(n*x) + b_n sin(n*x)
  a_n = 1/pi int f(x) cos(n*x) dx over the period, b_n likewise with sin
  a0 = 0
  a_n = 0 (f is odd)
  b_n = 2(1 - (-1)^n)/(pi*n)
  check: the closed forms equal the numeric coefficients (Gauss-Legendre, 5120 nodes) for n = 1..48
  f(x) ~ 4sin(x)/pi + 4sin(3x)/(3pi) + 4sin(5x)/(5pi) + 4sin(7x)/(7pi) + 4sin(9x)/(9pi) + ...
  partial sums S_N: ||f - S_N||/||f|| = 0.259 (N = 5), 0.0919 (N = 48)
  converges to f where f is continuous, and to the midpoint of the jump where it jumps:
    at x = 0 it jumps by 2: S_N -> 0
    at x = pi it jumps by -2: S_N -> the midpoint
  Gibbs: near x = 0, S_48 overshoots by 0.1791 = 9.0% of the jump (at x = 0.06545); the overshoot tends to 8.95% of the jump and does not shrink as N grows
> fourier series x^2
Fourier series of x^2 on [-pi, pi] (period 2pi):
  f(x) ~ a0/2 + sum_{n>=1} a_n cos(n*x) + b_n sin(n*x)
  a_n = 1/pi int f(x) cos(n*x) dx over the period, b_n likewise with sin
  a0 = 2pi^2/3
  a_n = 4(-1)^n/n^2
  b_n = 0 (f is even)
  check: the closed forms equal the numeric coefficients (Gauss-Legendre, 5120 nodes) for n = 1..48
  f(x) ~ pi^2/3 - 4cos(x) + cos(2x) - 4cos(3x)/9 + cos(4x)/4 - 4cos(5x)/25 + ...
  partial sums S_N: ||f - S_N||/||f|| = 0.0285 (N = 5), 0.0011 (N = 48)
  f is continuous as a periodic function: S_N -> f uniformly (no Gibbs overshoot)
> fourier series abs(x) on -1..1
Fourier series of abs(x) on [-1, 1] (period 2):
  f(x) ~ a0/2 + sum_{n>=1} a_n cos(pi*n*x) + b_n sin(pi*n*x)
  a_n = int f(x) cos(pi*n*x) dx over the period, b_n likewise with sin
  a0 = 1
  a_n = -2(1 - (-1)^n)/(pi^2*n^2)
  b_n = 0 (f is even)
  check: the closed forms equal the numeric coefficients (Gauss-Legendre, 5120 nodes) for n = 1..48
  f(x) ~ 1/2 - 4cos(pi*x)/(pi^2) - 4cos(3pi*x)/(9pi^2) - 4cos(5pi*x)/(25pi^2) - 4cos(7pi*x)/(49pi^2) - 4cos(9pi*x)/(81pi^2) + ...
  partial sums S_N: ||f - S_N||/||f|| = 0.0134 (N = 5), 0.000609 (N = 48)
  f is continuous as a periodic function: S_N -> f uniformly (no Gibbs overshoot)
> fourier series sin(x)^3
Fourier series of sin(x)^3 on [-pi, pi] (period 2pi):
  f(x) ~ a0/2 + sum_{n>=1} a_n cos(n*x) + b_n sin(n*x)
  a_n = 1/pi int f(x) cos(n*x) dx over the period, b_n likewise with sin
  a0 = 0
  finite: every coefficient after n = 3 is 0
  a_1 = 0, b_1 = 3/4
  a_3 = 0, b_3 = -1/4
  f(x) ~ 3sin(x)/4 - sin(3x)/4
  partial sums S_N: ||f - S_N||/||f|| = 1.29e-15 (N = 5), 1.29e-15 (N = 48)
  f is continuous as a periodic function: S_N -> f uniformly (no Gibbs overshoot)
> fourier series {1 if x < 0.5, 0 otherwise} on 0..1
Fourier series of x < 0.5 ? 1 : 0 on [0, 1] (period 1):
  f(x) ~ a0/2 + sum_{n>=1} a_n cos(2pi*n*x) + b_n sin(2pi*n*x)
  a_n = 2 int f(x) cos(2pi*n*x) dx over the period, b_n likewise with sin
  a0 = 1
  a_n = 0
  b_n = (1 - (-1)^n)/(pi*n)
  check: the closed forms equal the numeric coefficients (Gauss-Legendre, 5120 nodes) for n = 1..48
  f(x) ~ 1/2 + 2sin(2pi*x)/pi + 2sin(6pi*x)/(3pi) + 2sin(10pi*x)/(5pi) + 2sin(14pi*x)/(7pi) + 2sin(18pi*x)/(9pi) + ...
  partial sums S_N: ||f - S_N||/||f|| = 0.183 (N = 5), 0.065 (N = 48)
  converges to f where f is continuous, and to the midpoint of the jump where it jumps:
    at x = 1/2 it jumps by -1: S_N -> 1/2
    at x = 1 it jumps by 1: S_N -> the midpoint
  Gibbs: near x = 1/2, S_48 overshoots by 0.08956 = 9.0% of the jump (at x = 0.4896); the overshoot tends to 8.95% of the jump and does not shrink as N grows
> fourier e^(-x^2)
F{e^(-x^2)}(w) = sqrt(pi)*e^(-w^2/4)
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 6.7e-16
> fourier e^(-2abs(x))
F{e^(-2abs(x))}(w) = 4/(w^2 + 4)
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 9.6e-15
> fourier 1/(x^2 + 1)
F{1/(x^2 + 1)}(w) = pi*e^(-abs(w))
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 4.5e-10
> fourier {1 if abs(x) < 1, 0 otherwise}
F{abs(x) < 1 ? 1 : 0}(w) = 2sin(w)/w
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 5.8e-16
> fourier e^(-x) u(x)
F{e^(-x)*u(x)}(w) = 1/(w^2 + 1) - i*w/(w^2 + 1)
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 1.4e-14
> fourier x e^(-x^2)
F{x*e^(-x^2)}(w) = -i*sqrt(pi)*e^(-w^2/4)*w/2
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 7.4e-16
> fourier cos(3x) e^(-x^2)
F{cos(3x)*e^(-x^2)}(w) = (sqrt(pi)*e^(-(w - 3)^2/4) + sqrt(pi)*e^(-(w + 3)^2/4))/2
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 1.2e-15
> fourier cos(2x)
F{cos(2x)}(w) = pi*(delta(w - 2) + delta(w + 2))
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  delta terms: f does not decay, the transform is a distribution (not checked numerically)
> ifourier e^(-w^2)
F^-1{e^(-w^2)}(x) = e^(-x^2/4)/(2sqrt(pi))
  convention: f(x) = (1/(2pi)) int F(w) e^(iwx) dw
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at x = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 6.3e-16
