  center x = 2; coefficients a_n = 1/(n*3^n) of (x - 2)^(n)
  radius of convergence R = 3 (ratio test: |a_(n+1)/a_n| -> 1/3)
  at x = -1: converges conditionally (alternating series test (|a_n| decreases to 0); not absolutely: |a_n| ~ C/n (p <= 1))
  at x = 5: diverges (limit comparison with the harmonic series sum 1/n (p = 1): a_n n -> 1)
  interval of convergence: [-1, 5)
  sum = -log(1 - (x - 2)/3)    for |x - 2| < 3 (-log(1 - u))
> radius x^n/(n^2 2^n)
power series sum(n, 1, inf, x^n/(n^2*2^n)) in x
  center x = 0; coefficients a_n = 1/(n^2*2^n) of x^(n)
  radius of convergence R = 2 (ratio test: |a_(n+1)/a_n| -> 1/2)
  at x = -2: converges absolutely (|a_n| ~ C/n^2: comparison with a p-series (p > 1))
  at x = 2: converges absolutely (p-series 1/n^2 with p = 2 > 1)
  interval of convergence: [-2, 2]
> radius n! x^n
power series sum(n, 0, inf, n!*x^n) in x
  center x = 0; coefficients a_n = n! of x^(n)
  radius of convergence R = 0 (ratio test: |a_(n+1)/a_n| -> inf)
  converges only at x = 0
> powerseries e^(2x)
power series of e^(2x) about x = 0
  Taylor coefficients c_0..c_7: 1, 2, 2, 4/3, 2/3, 4/15, 4/45, 8/315
  = sum_{m>=0} c_m x^m, c_m = 2^m/m!
  matches e^u: radius of convergence inf
> powerseries sin(x)
power series of sin(x) about x = 0
  Taylor coefficients c_0..c_7: 0, 1, 0, -1/6, 0, 1/120, 0, -0.0001984126984
  = sum_{k>=0} (-1)^k/(2k + 1)! * x^(2k + 1)
  radius of convergence inf
> powerseries log(1 + x)
power series of log(x + 1) about x = 0
  Taylor coefficients c_0..c_7: 0, 1, -1/2, 1/3, -1/4, 1/5, -1/6, 1/7
  = sum_{m>=1} c_m x^m, c_m = -(-1)^m/m
  matches -log(1 - u): radius of convergence 1
> asymptotic sum(k, 1, n, 1/k)
sum(k, 1, n, 1/k) ~ log(n) + gamma + 1/(2n) - 1/(12n^2) + 1/(120n^4) + ...
  Euler-Maclaurin: int f + C + f(n)/2 + B2/2! f'(n) + B4/4! f'''(n), B2 = 1/6, B4 = -1/30
  the constant C = 0.577215664901532 = gamma
  check: n = 10: sum 2.92896825397, expansion off by 3.9e-09; n = 100: sum 5.18737751764, expansion off by 2.7e-15; n = 1000: sum 7.48547086055, expansion off by 0
> asymptotic sum(k, 1, n, log(k))
sum(k, 1, n, log(k)) ~ n*log(n) - n + log(n)/2 + log(2pi)/2 + 1/(12n) - 1/(360n^3) + ...
  Euler-Maclaurin: int f + C + f(n)/2 + B2/2! f'(n) + B4/4! f'''(n), B2 = 1/6, B4 = -1/30
  the constant C = 0.918938533202891 = log(2pi)/2
  check: n = 10: sum 15.1044125731, expansion off by 7.9e-09; n = 100: sum 363.739375556, expansion off by 1.9e-12; n = 1000: sum 5912.12817849, expansion off by 1e-11
> the laplace transform of t^2 e^-t
  ucas: laplace t^2 e^-t
L{t^2*e^(-t)}(s) = 2/(s + 1)^3
  region of convergence: Re(s) > -1
  one-sided: f(t) for t >= 0 (0 before)
  rules: e^(at) f(t) -> F(s - a), t^n f(t) -> (-1)^n F^(n)(s)
  check: matches int_0^inf f(t) e^(-st) dt (Gauss-Kronrod) at s = -1/2, 1/2, 3: max relative difference 4.4e-16
> inverse laplace of 1/(s^2+1)
  ucas: ilaplace 1/(s^2+1)
L^-1{1/(s^2 + 1)}(t) = sin(t)
  poles: s = +-i
  for t >= 0 (0 before): the right-sided inverse, region of convergence Re(s) > 0 (right of every pole)
  check: transformed back (Gauss-Kronrod), it matches F at s = 1, 2, 7/2: max relative difference 1.4e-16
> solve y'' + y = 0 with y(0) = 1, y'(0) = 0 using laplace
  ucas: laplace solve y'' + y = 0 with y(0) = 1, y'(0) = 0
y'' + y = 0, y(0) = 1, y'(0) = 0
  transformed: (s^2 + 1)Y(s) - (s) = 0
  Y(s) = s/(s^2 + 1)
  y(t) = cos(t)    for t >= 0
  poles of Y: +-i
  check: residual of the equation at 201 points of [0, 10]: max 0; initial values hold (max difference 0)
  check: a numeric solution (RK4, h = 1/1000) agrees at 20 points of [0, 10]: max difference 6.6e-14
> the z transform of a^n u[n]
  ucas: ztrans a^n u[n]
Z{a^n*u[n]}(z) = z/(z - a)
  region of convergence: |z| > abs(a)
  one-sided: x[n] for n >= 0
  rules: a^n x[n] -> X(z/a)
  check: matches sum_n x[n] z^-n (88 terms) at z = 17/10, 17/5, 71/10 with a = 7/10: max relative difference 2e-16
> inverse z transform of z/(z - 0.5)
  ucas: iztrans z/(z - 0.5)
Z^-1{z/(z - 1/2)}[n] = (1/2)^n    for n >= 0
  poles: z = 1/2
  taken as causal (right-sided): region of convergence |z| > 1/2
  long division in z^-1: 1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, 1/16, 1/32, 1/64, 1/128, ...
  check: the closed form equals the long division for n = 0..31 (max difference 0)
> transfer function of y[n] = 0.9 y[n-1] + x[n]
  ucas: tf y[n] = 0.9 y[n-1] + x[n]
transfer function of y[n] = 0.9 y[n-1] + x[n] (zero initial state)
  input x, output y: H(z) = Y(z)/X(z)
  H(z) = 10z/(10z - 9)
       = 1/(1 - (9/10)z^-1)
  poles: 9/10
  zeros: 0
  stable (BIBO): every pole inside the unit circle (max |p| = 9/10)
  causal: yes (deg numerator <= deg denominator)
  impulse response h[n] = (9/10)^n    for n >= 0
  DC gain H(1) = 10
  frequency response H(e^(iw)), w in radians per sample:
    w          |H|          dB         phase (deg)
    0          10           20         0
    pi/8       2.60805      8.326      -63.93
    pi/4       1.36436      2.699      -60.26
    pi/2       0.743294     -2.577     -41.99
    3pi/4      0.569545     -4.889     -21.25
    pi         0.526316     -5.575     0
  -3 dB (gain 10/sqrt(2)) at w = 0.105458 rad/sample
    = 0.0167842 of the sample rate
  as C (direct form I; x1 = x[n-1], y1 = y[n-1], ...):
    float y = x + 0.9f*y1;
    y1 = y;
> is the filter y[n] = 1.1 y[n-1] + x[n] stable?
  ucas: tf y[n] = 1.1 y[n-1] + x[n]
transfer function of y[n] = 1.1 y[n-1] + x[n] (zero initial state)
  input x, output y: H(z) = Y(z)/X(z)
  H(z) = 10z/(10z - 11)
       = 1/(1 - (11/10)z^-1)
  poles: 11/10
  zeros: 0
  UNSTABLE: a pole outside the unit circle (|p| = 11/10)
  causal: yes (deg numerator <= deg denominator)
  impulse response h[n] = (11/10)^n    for n >= 0
  DC gain H(1) = -10
  frequency response H(e^(iw)), w in radians per sample:
    w          |H|          dB         phase (deg)
    0          10           20         -180
    pi/8       2.3738       7.509      -92.21
    pi/4       1.2362       1.842      -74.06
    pi/2       0.672673     -3.444     -47.73
    3pi/4      0.515325     -5.758     -23.63
    pi         0.47619      -6.444     0
  (not BIBO stable, so no steady state: this is H on the unit circle, not what the system settles to)
  as C (direct form I; x1 = x[n-1], y1 = y[n-1], ...):
    float y = x + 1.1f*y1;
    y1 = y;
> fourier series of a square wave
  ucas: fourier series sign(x)
Fourier series of sign(x) on [-pi, pi] (period 2pi):
  f(x) ~ a0/2 + sum_{n>=1} a_n cos(n*x) + b_n sin(n*x)
  a_n = 1/pi int f(x) cos(n*x) dx over the period, b_n likewise with sin
  a0 = 0
  a_n = 0 (f is odd)
  b_n = 2(1 - (-1)^n)/(pi*n)
  check: the closed forms equal the numeric coefficients (Gauss-Legendre, 5120 nodes) for n = 1..48
  f(x) ~ 4sin(x)/pi + 4sin(3x)/(3pi) + 4sin(5x)/(5pi) + 4sin(7x)/(7pi) + 4sin(9x)/(9pi) + ...
  partial sums S_N: ||f - S_N||/||f|| = 0.259 (N = 5), 0.0919 (N = 48)
  converges to f where f is continuous, and to the midpoint of the jump where it jumps:
    at x = 0 it jumps by 2: S_N -> 0
    at x = pi it jumps by -2: S_N -> the midpoint
  Gibbs: near x = 0, S_48 overshoots by 0.1791 = 9.0% of the jump (at x = 0.06545); the overshoot tends to 8.95% of the jump and does not shrink as N grows
> fourier transform of e^(-x^2)
  ucas: fourier e^(-x^2)
F{e^(-x^2)}(w) = sqrt(pi)*e^(-w^2/4)
  convention: F(w) = int f(x) e^(-iwx) dx over the real line (angular frequency; the inverse has 1/(2pi))
  check: matches the integral computed numerically at w = 1/2, 13/10, 27/10: max relative difference 6.7e-16
> does the sum of 1/n^2 converge
  ucas: converge 1/n^2
sum(n, 1, inf, 1/n^2)
  converges absolutely: p-series 1/n^2 with p = 2 > 1
  partial sums: S_10 = 1.549767731, S_1000 = 1.643934567
> what is the sum of 1/n^2 from 1 to infinity
  ucas: sum(n, 1, inf, 1/n^2)
sum(n, 1, inf, 1/n^2)
  converges absolutely: p-series 1/n^2 with p = 2 > 1
  = pi^2/6
  method: partial fractions; zeta(k), digamma at integers
  numeric: 1.644934066848226 (the first 128 terms plus an Euler-Maclaurin tail (integral + 3 corrections); estimated error 4.4e-16)
  check: the closed form and the numeric value agree (difference 4.4e-16)
> radius of convergence of the sum of x^n/n!
  ucas: radius x^n/n!
power series sum(n, 0, inf, x^n/n!) in x
  center x = 0; coefficients a_n = 1/n! of x^(n)
  radius of convergence R = inf (ratio test: |a_(n+1)/a_n| -> 0)
  interval of convergence: every x (absolutely)
  sum = e^x    for |x| < inf (e^u)
> the generating function of fibonacci
  ucas: genfunc fibonacci
fibonacci: a[n+2] = a[n+1] + a[n], a[0] = 0, a[1] = 1
  a[n] = sqrt(5)*((1 + sqrt(5))/2)^n/5 - sqrt(5)*((1 - sqrt(5))/2)^n/5
  G(x) = sum_{n>=0} a_n x^n = x/(1 - x - x^2)    for |x| < (-1 + sqrt(5))/2
  check: sum_n a_n x^n at x = (-1 + sqrt(5))/4 is 0.518927630227216, G = 0.518927630227215 (difference 1.1e-16)
> the product of 1 - 1/n^2 for n from 2 to infinity
  ucas: prod(n, 2, inf, 1 - 1/n^2)
prod(n, 2, inf, 1 - 1/n^2)
  converges: the factors are 1 + O(1/n^2) (sum |a_n - 1| converges)
  = 1/2
  method: the factors as (n - a_i)/(n - b_i): prod Gamma(2 - b_i)/Gamma(2 - a_i)
  numeric: 0.500000000058105 (partial products to 4097, Richardson in 1/N twice; estimated error 6e-08)
  check: agree (difference 5.8e-11)
> the asymptotic expansion of the sum of 1/k
  ucas: asymptotic sum(k, 1, n, 1/k)
sum(k, 1, n, 1/k) ~ log(n) + gamma + 1/(2n) - 1/(12n^2) + 1/(120n^4) + ...
  Euler-Maclaurin: int f + C + f(n)/2 + B2/2! f'(n) + B4/4! f'''(n), B2 = 1/6, B4 = -1/30
  the constant C = 0.577215664901532 = gamma
  check: n = 10: sum 2.92896825397, expansion off by 3.9e-09; n = 100: sum 5.18737751764, expansion off by 2.7e-15; n = 1000: sum 7.48547086055, expansion off by 0
